 "result": "**How they get in**\n\n- `_base_template_vars()` in `src/tessel/templating.py:21` is the default context processor. It is pre-registered under key `None` in `template_ctx_injectors` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184`). It supplies `g` and, when there is a request, `request`. `session` is not added there: the `session` proxy is left as is, so Jinja reads it through the proxy (see the comment at `templating.py:30`).\n- `render_template`, `render_template_string`, `stream_template` and `stream_template_string` all go through `_render` or `_stream` in `templating.py`. These call `App.fill_template_scope(ctx, context)` (`src/tessel/app.py:588`).\n- `fill_template_scope` runs every injector registered for `None`, then those for the current request's bundles. Each injector's returned dict updates the context, and the kwargs you passed to `render_template` are re-applied last, so they win.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n\nUse the `@ctx_injector` decorator (`Scaffold.ctx_injector`, `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`). It is available on both the app and bundles:\n\n```python\n@app.ctx_injector\ndef inject_user():\n    return {\"current_user\": ...}\n```\n\n- On the app, the injector runs for every template.\n- On a bundle, it runs only for templates rendered from that bundle's views. Use `Bundle.app_ctx_injector` to affect all templates from a bundle. I did not open the bundle file to confirm that method.",
